pci: dwc: round up the number of MSI control blocks

Both dw_pcie_setup_rc() and dw_handle_msi_irq() computed the number of
32-vector MSI control blocks with a plain division:

	num_ctrls = pp->num_vectors / MAX_MSI_IRQS_PER_CTRL;

RK3528 reports 8 MSI vectors (rk_pcie_msi_set_num_vectors()), so the
division yields 0 and both loops never execute:

 - dw_pcie_setup_rc() does not program PCIE_MSI_INTR0_ENABLE, which then
   keeps its reset value (all MSI vectors disabled), so the root complex
   quietly drops every MSI/MSI-X memory write issued by an endpoint;
 - dw_handle_msi_irq() reads no PCIE_MSI_INTR0_STATUS register at all, so
   nothing would be dispatched even if the interrupt had fired.

On the HINLINK OPC-H28K (RTL8111H behind the PCIe root complex) this shows
up as: link up, chip DMAs packets, MSI-X table properly programmed with the
root complex MSI target, yet the NIC driver never receives a TxOK / RxOK /
link-change interrupt.  Its tx queue then times out after 5s and the device
is reset in a loop, and /proc/interrupts never counts a single MSI.

Use DIV_ROUND_UP() so that a partially filled control block is still
initialised and polled, as the vendor 5.10 BSP does.
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware-host.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware-host.c
@@ -83,7 +83,11 @@
 	u32 status, num_ctrls;
 	irqreturn_t ret = IRQ_NONE;
 
-	num_ctrls = pp->num_vectors / MAX_MSI_IRQS_PER_CTRL;
+	/* RK3528 reports 8 MSI vectors, i.e. less than one 32-vector control
+	 * block, so a partially filled block must still be initialised and
+	 * polled.  See dw_pcie_setup_rc().
+	 */
+	num_ctrls = DIV_ROUND_UP(pp->num_vectors, MAX_MSI_IRQS_PER_CTRL);
 
 	for (i = 0; i < num_ctrls; i++) {
 		status = dw_pcie_readl_dbi(pci, PCIE_MSI_INTR0_STATUS +
@@ -679,7 +683,12 @@
 	dw_pcie_setup(pci);
 
 	if (!pp->ops->msi_host_init) {
-		num_ctrls = pp->num_vectors / MAX_MSI_IRQS_PER_CTRL;
+		/* DIV_ROUND_UP: see dw_handle_msi_irq().  With fewer than 32
+		 * vectors this loop used to be skipped entirely, leaving
+		 * PCIE_MSI_INTR0_ENABLE at its reset value (all vectors
+		 * disabled), which made the controller drop every MSI.
+		 */
+		num_ctrls = DIV_ROUND_UP(pp->num_vectors, MAX_MSI_IRQS_PER_CTRL);
 
 		/* Initialize IRQ Status array */
 		for (ctrl = 0; ctrl < num_ctrls; ctrl++) {
